❓Question
Given an integer array nums sorted in non-decreasing order, remove the duplicates in-place so that each unique element appears only once.
Return the number of unique elements k.
The first k elements of nums should contain the unique numbers in sorted order. The elements after index k - 1 can be ignored.

Example
Input: nums = [1,1,2]
Output: 2
Array after modification:
[1,2,2]
Only the first 2 elements matter: [1,2].

✅Approach — Two Pointers
p1 points to the last unique element.
p2 scans the array to find new unique elements.
If nums[p1] != nums[p2], we found a new unique number.
Put it at p1 + 1.
Move p1 forward.
Always move p2 forward.

Code
class Solution {
    public int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {
        int p1 = 0;
        int p2 = 1;
        int count = 1;
        while (p2 < nums.length) {
            if (nums[p1] != nums[p2]) {
                nums[p1 + 1] = nums[p2];
                p1++;
                count++;
            }
            p2++;
        }
        return count;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)
